Southern Denmark"],"title":"Diabetic Retinopathy as a Potential Marker of Protection of Prevalent and Incident Migraine: Results from a National Cohort Study","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-631237","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Progressive degeneration of dopaminergic neurons, immune activation, and α-synuclein pathology characterize Parkinson’s disease (PD). We previously reported that unilateral intrani-gral injection of recombinant adeno-associated viral (rAAV) vectors encoding wild-type human α-synuclein produced a rat model of early PD with dopamine terminal dysfunction. Here we tested the hypothesis that decreases in dopamine result in increased postsynaptic dopamine D2/D3 receptor expression, neuroinflammation, and reduced synaptic vesicle glycoprotein 2A (SV2A) density. Rats were injected with rAAV encoding α-synuclein or green fluorescent protein and subjected to non-pharmacological motor tests, before euthanization at 12 weeks post-injection. We performed: (1) in situ hybridization of nigral tyrosine hydroxylase mRNA, (2) HPLC of striatal dopamine content, and (3) autoradiography with [3 H]raclopride, [3 H]DTBZ, [3 H]GBR12935, [3 H]PK11195, and [3 H]UCB-J to measure binding at D2/3 receptors, vesicular monoamine transporter 2, dopamine transporters, mito-chondrial translocator protein, and SV2A, respectively. rAAV-α-synuclein induced motor asymmetry and reduced tyrosine hydroxylase mRNA and dopamine content in ipsilateral brain regions. This was paralleled by elevated ipsilateral postsynaptic dopamine D2/3 receptor expression and immune activation, with no changes to synaptic SV2A density. In conclusion, α-synuclein overexpression results in dopaminergic degeneration that induced compensatory increases in D2/3 binding and immune activation, recapitulating many of the pathological characteristics of PD.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Stokholm, Kathrine","Thomsen, Majken","Phan, Jenny-Ann","Møller, Line K.","Bay-Richter, Cecilie","Christiansen, Søren H.","Woldbye, David P.D.","Romero-Ramos, Marina","Landau, Anne M."],"cuid":["S02-097.50-025269"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Landau, Anne M."],"keyword":["Adeno associated viral vectors","Alpha synuclein","Autoradiography","Dopamine","Neuroinflammation","Parkinson s disease","Synaptic vesicle glycoprotein 2A","α Synuclein"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"α synuclein overexpression increases dopamine d000000000002 000000000003 receptor binding and immune activation in a model of early parkinson s disease","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/biomedicines9121876","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-097.50-025269","identifier":[{"id":"000736348300001","type":"wos"},{"id":"85121740483","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC8698691","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"34944691","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-ad476cb4-6e64-4541-839a-03d1e75ba88e","ku-7c6b35a5-cc20-4e9c-bedd-b5b2c1a8a6e3"],"keyword":["Adeno-associated viral vectors","Autoradiography","Dopamine","Neuroinflam-mation","Parkinson’s disease","Synaptic vesicle glycoprotein 2A","alpha-synuclein","α-synuclein"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-19A7C5687CC3BA48C77F310A6DA0F5E1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/288913928/_Synuclein.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/biomedicines9121876","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Biomedicine","Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Nuclear Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Neuroscience, Neuropharm and Genetics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kathrine","key":"stokholmpau","last":"Stokholm","locid":"43ec388f-6ebf-4309-bfa3-c886e94a922c","name":"Stokholm, Kathrine","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Majken Borup","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"0274d78a-0287-4cdd-b350-15a6939cf875","name":"Thomsen, Majken","name_org":"Thomsen, Majken Borup","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jenny Ann","key":"phanpau","last":"Phan","locid":"3bdb9c02-abe7-426f-b6c6-f27071522813","name":"Phan, Jenny-Ann","name_org":"Phan, Jenny Ann","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line K.","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","name":"Møller, Line K.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Line K.","key":"mollerpau","last":"Moller","name":"Moller, Line K.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Cecilie","key":"bayrichterpau","last":"Bay-Richter","locid":"5b92057b-0f79-4ff8-8a5b-742912aa23b7","name":"Bay-Richter, Cecilie","orcid":"0000-0002-2254-4020","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Søren H.","key":"christiansenpau","last":"Christiansen","locid":"24c3d63b-90c9-4bb3-810b-a1ca7badf896","name":"Christiansen, Søren Hofmann","name_org":"Christiansen, Søren H.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"David P. D.","key":"woldbyepau","last":"Woldbye","locid":"bc523287-caff-4eb5-932c-990fa1e1ac90","name":"Woldbye, David Paul Drucker","name_org":"Woldbye, David P. D.","orcid":"0000-0002-6321-3850","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Marina","key":"romeroramospau","last":"Romero-Ramos","locid":"4d964ab5-ac0c-44fd-9ab0-4c4cffbdfb8c","name":"Romero-Ramos, Marina","orcid":"0000-0003-0970-578X","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anne M.","key":"landaupau","last":"Landau","locid":"8ee0570a-d317-43ec-a3ba-8fb66cee875e","name":"Landau, Anne M.","orcid":"0000-0002-7371-8713","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34944691"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/biomedicines9121876","issn":["22279059"],"issue":"12","journal":"Biomedicines","paperid":"1876","publisher":"MDPI","status":"p","title":"Biomedicines","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85121740483"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-ad476cb4-6e64-4541-839a-03d1e75ba88e","ku-7c6b35a5-cc20-4e9c-bedd-b5b2c1a8a6e3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"α-Synuclein Overexpression Increases Dopamine D2/3 Receptor Binding and Immune Activation in a Model of Early Parkinson’s Disease","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-097.50-025269","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Environmental exposures during pregnancy that alter both the maternal gut microbiome and the infant's risk of allergic disease and asthma include a traditional farm environment and consumption of unpasteurized cow's milk, antibiotic use, dietary fiber and psychosocial stress. Multiple mechanisms acting in concert may underpin these associations and prime the infant to acquire immune competence and homeostasis following exposure to the extrauterine environment. Cellular and metabolic products of the maternal gut microbiome can promote the expression of microbial pattern recognition receptors, as well as thymic and bone marrow hematopoiesis relevant to regulatory immunity. At birth, transmission of maternally derived bacteria likely leverages this in utero programming to accelerate postnatal transition from a Th2 to Th1 and Th17 dominant immune phenotypes and maturation of regulatory immune mechanisms, which in turn reduce the child's risk of allergic disease and asthma. Although our understanding of these phenomena is rapidly evolving, the field is relatively nascent, and we are yet to translate existing knowledge into interventions that substantially reduce disease risk in humans. Here we review evidence that the maternal gut microbiome impacts the offspring's risk of allergic disease and asthma, discuss challenges and future directions for the field, and propose the hypothesis that maternal carriage of Prevotella copri during pregnancy decreases the offspring's risk of allergic disease via production of succinate which in turn promotes bone marrow myelopoiesis of dendritic cell precursors in the fetus.","author_count":20,"contributors":["Gao, Yuan","Nanan, Ralph","Macia, Laurence","Tan, Jian","Sominsky, Luba","Quinn, Thomas P.","O'Hely, Martin","Ponsonby, Anne-Louise","Tang, Mimi Lk","Collier, Fiona","Strickland, Deborah H.","Dhar, Poshmaal","Brix, Susanne","Phipps, Simon","Sly, Peter D.","Ranganathan, Sarath","Stokholm, Jakob","Kristiansen, Karsten","Gray, Lawrence","Vuillermin, Peter"],"cuid":["S02-099.84-037341"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, East","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Gray, Lawrence","Vuillermin, Peter"],"country":["Australia","China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI","Barwon Health","China National GeneBank","Deakin University","Florey Institute of Neuroscience and Mental Health","Murdoch Children's Research Institute","QIMR Berghofer Medical Research Institute","Royal Children's Hospital","Royal Children's Hospital Melbourne","Telethon Kids Institute","University of Melbourne","University of Queensland","University of Sydney","University of Western Australia"],"keyword":["Allergy","Asthma","Fetal immunity","Gut microbiome"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the maternal gut microbiome during pregnancy and offspring allergy and asthma","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/255432135/1_s2.0_S0091674921011301_main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-099.84-037341","identifier":[{"id":"34310928","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85113312173","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-d214cf4b-9d36-467c-af5c-7d8ac31f9b81","ku-d05a372c-0c7c-493f-9c3e-9d59810570a5"],"keyword":["Allergy","Asthma","Fetal immunity","Gut microbiome"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-4661FF1BCB3DBF12E09A8FF402E97BBE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/33426